The cause is in `db.js:11-16`: `withClient` calls `client.release()` after `fn(client)`, with no try/finally. Any error thrown inside `fn` skips the release and that connection is gone from the pool permanently. Fix:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

**I wouldn't raise the pool size yet. The code has a connection leak, and a bigger pool would only take longer to run out.**

### The bug

In `db.js:11-16`, `withClient` only releases the client if `fn` succeeds:

```js
const client = await pool.connect();
const result = await fn(client);   // if this throws...
client.release();                  // ...this never runs
```

Every failed query or thrown callback keeps one of the 20 connections checked out forever. Once enough errors pile up, nothing is left.

The fix is to release in a `finally`:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

If callers run transactions inside `fn`, one more tweak helps. Call `client.release(err)` when there's an error so the pool throws that connection away. Otherwise it goes back to the pool possibly stuck mid-transaction.

### What the log shows (and doesn't)

- `idle=0` on every line while `waiting` climbs steadily from 7 to 28. Connections are never coming back, which fits a leak. It would also fit load that simply exceeds capacity, so the log alone can't tell them apart. The strong evidence is in the code.
- The file has only 7 lines, covering about 2.5 minutes (14:02:11–14:04:42), not an hour. If you have more history, it's worth checking whether `idle` ever goes above 0 or stays pinned even when traffic is low. Pinned at low traffic points to a leak.

### How to confirm it's a leak

While the pool is maxed out, run `SELECT state, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE usename = '<app user>' GROUP BY state;`. Many connections showing `idle` on the Postgres side while your pool reports `idle=0` means the app is holding connections it isn't using: leaked clients.

### Caveat

I could only see `db.js`. Any code that calls `pool.connect()` directly, without going through `withClient`, needs the same `try/finally`. Once the leak is fixed, if you still hit the limit under real load, that's the time to size the pool. Stay well below Postgres's `max_connections`, divided across all app instances.

